WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.160
Today, we’re gonna be taking a look at one
of the odder SCPs in the Foundation’s custody.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.750
The Foundation has plenty of weird and downright
silly anomalies, as we’ve covered extensively

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:11.750
in the past.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:16.490
Plenty of them are objects that you wouldn’t
imagine need to be under heavy containment,

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:17.890
but you’d be wrong.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:23.090
Even innocuous household objects with a harmless,
albeit annoying anomaly can pose an immense

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:26.030
danger to normalcy and human life.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:32.609
So today we’re gonna be taking a look at
SCP-2966, affectionately and nervously referred

00:00:32.609 --> 00:00:38.040
to by scientists on break and at the water
cooler with an odd nickname: the Infinite

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.040
TP.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.679
TP, of course, refers to exactly what you’re
thinking: toilet paper.

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:47.879
Just based off that nickname, you’ve probably
already gotten a pretty good idea of what

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:50.999
this anomaly is, but you’d be surprised.

00:00:50.999 --> 00:00:56.460
Just because something is predictable doesn’t
mean its harmless, or easy to contain.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:03.389
SCP-2966 is contained at Outpost 117, a small,
isolated little facility far, far out in the

00:01:03.389 --> 00:01:08.840
icy tundras of northern Canada, well away
from any other settlements or Foundation sites.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:15.420
It’s kept so far away because any other
SCP objects coming into contact with SCP-2966

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.240
is incredibly dangerous, and prohibited under
any circumstances.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.909
It’s also kept so far away from cities or
towns for another reason - below the ground,

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:30.469
Outpost 117 is host to an experimental thorium
nuclear reactor.

00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:35.020
Thorium reactors are highly efficient nuclear
reactors, but only a few exist in the entire

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.859
world for scientists to perform research upon.

00:01:37.859 --> 00:01:42.130
What’s one doing under a tiny, out of the
way facility in the Canadian wilderness?

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:47.350
Well, all nuclear reactors produce a lot of
heat and energy, as they’re designed to.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:53.630
This reactor is right next to the containment
chamber SCP-2966 is locked in, pumping heat

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.619
into the chamber at incredibly high temperatures.

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:03.689
SCP-2966 can only be removed for experimentation,
study, or cleaning when enough heat has been

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:07.369
pumped into the chamber to heavily reduce
how much matter there is.

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:12.060
But the SCP Foundation is in the business
of containing anomalies, not destroying them

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.680
like the GOC.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.890
Why would they be using nuclear power to slowly
and constantly degrade what seems like a fairly

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.590
innocuous anomaly?

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:24.660
Well, clearly it’s not entirely innocuous.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.830
Outpost 117’s staff is as barebones as the
facility itself: only a single skeleton crew

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:35.400
of 16 people, just enough to keep the facility
and its reactor up and running without risking

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.100
any casualties.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:41.880
Three nuclear engineers trained in the upkeep
of nuclear reactors, and four Foundation researchers

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.970
to monitor the reactor and SCP-2966’s containment
chamber.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:51.081
Along with that, there are 8 trained soldiers
from the Foundation’s MTFs to guard the

00:02:51.081 --> 00:02:55.300
facility and its perimeter 25km out from the
outpost itself.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.280
But that’s only 15 people - who’s the
16th?

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:04.380
Well, unsurprisingly, it’s a D-class subject,
to be used for direct experimentation with

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:05.620
the anomaly.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:10.370
Considering most SCP sites have personnel
counts in the hundreds of even thousands,

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:13.020
16 seems remarkably little.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:18.920
But the Foundation has learned their lesson
from past breach events involving SCP-2966.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.000
The D-class in the orange jumpsuit has an
extremely important job, though.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:29.700
Every eight hours, three times a day, the
D-class is to ‘use’ SCP-2966.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.700
Given what we know the anomaly is… you can
imagine what that entails.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:38.420
But whenever it is used, at least ten sheets
of toilet paper have to be removed from it

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.400
and, well, used.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.490
That use is heavily monitored by the research
team and a few of the MTF troopers, and radio

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.550
communication is maintained throughout the
whole gross event.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:52.090
The research team advises the D-class on whether
to speed up or slow down the rate at which

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:54.830
they tear off paper from SCP-2966.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:04:00.370
There’s other equipment, too: a vast array
of sensors and scales to measure SCP-2966’s

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:07.030
mass and density, and the data is fed back
to the researchers and into the nuclear reactor’s

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.030
systems.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:11.440
Needless to say, all of this is extremely
strange.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.790
What could a roll of TP, infinite as it may
be, possibly do to warrant a dedicated research

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.660
team and nuclear reactor?

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:23.560
Not to mention regular use experiments and
constant monitoring of its data points?

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.940
Whatever it is, it’s dangerous.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.550
The Foundation is currently constructing a
runway for jet aircraft just outside of Outpost

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:37.270
117, so that personnel can evacuate should
something go critical - not the nuclear reactor,

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:39.320
but SCP-2966.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:45.060
Clearly, whatever it can do, it’s more worrisome
than a nuclear reactor going into meltdown.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.930
We’ve got a lot of questions by now, so
let’s see if we can figure out what exactly

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.910
SCP-2966 does.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:59.250
Our predictions from the name, however, were
entirely accurate: SCP-2966 is an anomalous

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.280
branded roll of toilet paper.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:04.960
For security reasons, we can’t tell you
the brand, but we can tell you that it’s

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.620
entirely possible SCP-2966 might have a distant
cousin in your own bathroom.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.940
The first strange thing about SCP-2966 is
its weight.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.470
It weighs at least several kilograms, though
its weight is usually changing and Foundation

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:23.640
personnel are told not to try reducing it,
not after the incident.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.450
Anyway, it sticks around 5 and a half kilograms.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:31.420
You’ll notice that’s a lot heavier than
you’d expect a toilet paper roll to be,

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.900
given it’s made of… well, paper.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.730
Its weight isn’t the only unusual thing
about it: it’s also really good toilet paper.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:40.889
Anomalously good, even.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:44.980
We won’t go into detail because I don’t
think anyone wants the details on that, but

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:49.200
let’s just say - the best toilet paper you’ve
ever used?

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.380
SCP-2966 is softer, stronger, better in every
way.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:59.550
But once a sheet is ripped out of SCP-2966’s
roll, it’s not anomalous anymore, and can

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.500
be used and thrown away normally.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:07.820
SCP-2966 itself is attached to the wall inside
a metal housing, the kind you’d see in a

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:13.670
public bathroom, with no way to remove or
replace it - not that it needs to be replaced.

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:17.889
X-rays and ultrasounds of the metal housing
are inconclusive, but the Foundation doesn't

00:06:17.889 --> 00:06:21.240
want to cut into it with saws because… well,
you’ll see.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:25.410
Not to mention the fact that the metal housing
is the main way the reactor’s heat is funneled

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:31.120
into SCP-2966, and playing with nuclear power
is a little risky.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.260
But what’s actually anomalous about SCP-2966?

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:38.700
Well, it’s an infinite roll of toilet paper.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.220
But we knew that already.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:45.190
Since SCP-2966 was contained, at least 10
kilometres of toilet paper have been pulled

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:48.100
from it – over 6 miles!

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:52.430
The research team hasn’t seen any evidence
of it absorbing mass or producing new toilet

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:56.250
paper, and testing has revealed it’s not
pulling the paper from an alternate dimension

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.910
or reality.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:03.600
Despite all the testing, the researchers are
baffled by how exactly SCP-2966 keeps producing

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.490
the toilet paper that it does.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:09.720
But even if they don’t understand how it
happens, they have gained a pretty good understanding

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.040
of what the results are.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:15.380
And the results are a lot less fun than you
might think.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:22.120
See, SCP-2966 still obeys the first law of
thermodynamics: matter and energy can’t

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:26.060
be created or destroyed, only change forms.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:32.520
So SCP-2966 doesn’t actually create toilet
paper so much as it absorbs energy from its

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:38.540
surroundings, then transforms that energy
into matter - more specifically, toilet paper.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:43.530
How exactly it absorbs energy is unclear to
the Foundation, but they’ve run the math.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:49.540
One sheet of SCP-2966 weighs approximately
three grams, which means that approximately

00:07:49.540 --> 00:08:00.400
2.04×1016 joules of energy, or roughly 65
tons of TNT, is needed for one sheet of SCP-2966

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.630
to be produced.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:06.820
From there, they were able to extrapolate
that SCP-2966 contains at least several thousand

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:13.790
sheets within the roll - an extremely, extremely
large amount of explosive power, all contained

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:16.010
in a roll of toilet paper.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:20.030
Now you get why SCP-2966 is so dangerous.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:24.620
It’s not just a roll of toilet paper - in
the wrong hands, it could be one of the most

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:29.750
powerful explosives ever detonated, easily
able to wipe out half a country.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:35.269
But if you think that’s where SCP-2966’s
danger really lies, think again.

00:08:35.269 --> 00:08:41.229
Removing SCP-2966’s sheets is harmless,
until you remove ten of them at a time.

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:46.129
Then it’ll start absorbing the energy around
it, at roughly the rate that sheets are removed.

00:08:46.129 --> 00:08:51.420
It’s trying to rapidly gain energy to rebuild
itself, and it’ll get the energy from anything.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:56.670
It’s usually in the form of heat energy,
but it’ll absorb anything, even sound!

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:02.889
But when there’s no active source of power,
SCP-2966 becomes dangerous.

00:09:02.889 --> 00:09:07.199
It starts sucking up energy from the ambient
heat around it, and given the large amount

00:09:07.199 --> 00:09:09.779
of energy, it sucks it up fast.

00:09:09.779 --> 00:09:16.519
In only a few minutes, it can bring the temperature
of the room its in to absolute zero, completely

00:09:16.519 --> 00:09:18.730
stopping all molecular activity.

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:24.259
This has happened twice when the power went
out at Outpost 117: both times, the casualties

00:09:24.259 --> 00:09:25.579
were high.

00:09:25.579 --> 00:09:30.519
Research personnel sacrificed themselves to
run into SCP-2966’s chamber and start ripping

00:09:30.519 --> 00:09:33.470
sheets away from it, bringing the temperature
down.

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:39.890
They all died from hypothermia, but they saved
the entire site and stopped SCP-2966 from

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:41.029
going ‘critical’.

00:09:41.029 --> 00:09:47.149
You see, when SCP-2966 isn’t used for a
while, it starts to decay.

00:09:47.149 --> 00:09:52.649
The matter its gained in the form of toilet
paper is reduced back into pure energy: radiation.

00:09:52.649 --> 00:09:57.759
Thankfully, it’s not ionizing radiation,
the kind you’d find in a nuclear bomb.

00:09:57.759 --> 00:10:03.399
It’s simple heat and alpha radiation, mostly
harmless if you’re equipped right.

00:10:03.399 --> 00:10:07.649
After extensive testing and more than a little
trial and error, the researchers figured out

00:10:07.649 --> 00:10:13.399
the perfect distance between using SCP-2966
after letting it absorb energy, and letting

00:10:13.399 --> 00:10:16.130
it decay into radiation: 8 hours.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:24.360
That’s why the D-class has to use SCP-2966
every 8 hours, because if it goes over, something

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.200
very bad happens.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:33.800
SCP-2966 decays matter and energy exponentially,
and gets faster and faster the longer its

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:35.339
left untouched.

00:10:35.339 --> 00:10:41.480
Within a day and a half, SCP-2966 goes ‘critical’,
at which point the rate of energy radiation

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:45.889
gets so high that it starts rapidly getting
close to infinity.

00:10:45.889 --> 00:10:48.670
Sounds like a lot of complicated physics,
right?

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:51.779
So let’s bring it down to terms all of us
will understand.

00:10:51.779 --> 00:10:58.970
When the rate of energy decay becomes infinite,
what happens is that SCP-2966’s entire mass

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.529
is converted into energy in a single moment.

00:11:02.529 --> 00:11:08.649
All that energy stockpile is released in one
gigantic, titanic explosion.

00:11:08.649 --> 00:11:14.110
Remember how we mentioned that SCP-2966 could
feasibly be used as a bomb?

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.470
Yeah, this is that bomb exploding.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:20.529
The explosion would result in at least 1000
megatons of TNT.

00:11:20.529 --> 00:11:24.880
For comparison, the Tsar Bomba, the Soviet
“mother of all bombs”, had a yield of

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:27.110
about 50 megatons.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:33.430
Needless to say, SCP-2966 going critical and
exploding would very likely be an XK-Class

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:34.959
Doomsday event.

00:11:34.959 --> 00:11:40.100
Aside from blowing up half of Canada, it would
cause wildfires, tsunamis, and easily blanket

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.509
the sky in ash and soot, choking out plant
life.

00:11:43.509 --> 00:11:46.869
And that’s all aside from the massive human
casualties.

00:11:46.869 --> 00:11:52.139
The Foundation has long since realized that
even though Outpost 117 is out of the way,

00:11:52.139 --> 00:11:57.639
SCP-2966 going off would be much, much more
than even they could handle.

00:11:57.639 --> 00:12:03.829
Thus, the containment procedures currently
in place: keep SCP-2966 right in the sweet

00:12:03.829 --> 00:12:08.760
spot so that it doesn’t need to absorb energy
from people, but make sure it doesn’t get

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:14.810
enough energy that it decides today is a great
day to extinguish human life as we know it.

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:20.399
When SCP-2966 was being used by the D-class,
they noticed something a little odd.

00:12:20.399 --> 00:12:24.579
The D-class, from the toilet on their radios,
reported that the room temperature of the

00:12:24.579 --> 00:12:26.899
containment chamber seemed to drop a little.

00:12:26.899 --> 00:12:31.089
The researchers aren’t quite sure of what
this means yet, but the D-class also reported

00:12:31.089 --> 00:12:38.420
that the paper removed from SCP-2966 is exceptionally
comfortable, strong, and effective as toilet

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:39.420
paper.

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:45.059
A handful of the researchers submitted a proposal:
to remove sheets of toilet paper from SCP-2966

00:12:45.059 --> 00:12:49.480
and reroll them to send to other Foundation
facilities.

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:56.070
But due to the risk and possibility of accidentally
creating another SCP-2966, the Council denied

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:57.500
it out of hand.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:03.569
So now we’ve got a much better understanding
of what the heck SCP-2966 is, and how a little

00:13:03.569 --> 00:13:09.360
roll of toilet paper could pose such a massive,
overwhelming threat to not just the Foundation,

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.670
but all human life.

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:15.700
And why the containment procedures are as
extensive and thought-out as they are.

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:20.290
The research teams at Outpost 117 are hard
at work performing additional research on

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:27.949
SCP-2966, but there are still big questions:
most importantly, how was this thing created?

00:13:27.949 --> 00:13:29.209
Who created it?

00:13:29.209 --> 00:13:33.239
And how did the Foundation get ahold of it
and learn what it could do without accidentally

00:13:33.239 --> 00:13:34.689
detonating it?

00:13:34.689 --> 00:13:39.559
Well, SCP-2966 was discovered after global
weather satellites noticed the temperature

00:13:39.559 --> 00:13:42.470
dropping in a small part of the Canadian wilderness.

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:47.329
The Foundation, being tapped into the global
information network, also noticed, and realized

00:13:47.329 --> 00:13:49.310
the temperature drop was anomalous.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:53.689
Agents in the area confirmed that something
strange was going on, and Mobile Task Force

00:13:53.689 --> 00:13:59.439
Theta-19, the Rocketeers, found a small research
facility named “Pine Range Research” out

00:13:59.439 --> 00:14:00.629
in the backwoods.

00:14:00.629 --> 00:14:05.839
But that wasn’t the strange part - the strange
part was how it was completely frozen over

00:14:05.839 --> 00:14:10.699
for an entire square kilometer, despite it
being a warm summer day.

00:14:10.699 --> 00:14:15.550
The MTF measured the temperature, then rechecked
it three more times to ensure they hadn’t

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:20.560
gone insane - the temperature was -108 degrees
celsius.

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:25.779
For comparison, the coldest temperature ever
recorded on earth was -90 degrees Celsius.

00:14:25.779 --> 00:14:29.949
The research team are now able to say that
the only reason everything wasn’t at absolute

00:14:29.949 --> 00:14:35.290
zero was because the breach had happened hours
ago, and the sun had time to warm everything

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:36.299
up a little.

00:14:36.299 --> 00:14:40.759
The Mobile Task Force breached into the building,
and found a scene of devastation.

00:14:40.759 --> 00:14:44.910
All the individuals in the building had been
killed from hypothermia and exposure.

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.749
As they went further in, they encountered
a lecture hall, with one researcher giving

00:14:48.749 --> 00:14:52.850
a lecture to others holding SCP-2966.

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:59.439
Because SCP-2966 had completely frozen everything,
all the electronics in the building were corrupted,

00:14:59.439 --> 00:15:02.110
and the Foundation couldn’t get any useful
data.

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:07.310
But the MTF did discover a handwritten journal
by the researcher in one of the offices, and

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:10.910
were able to recover small sections of text
after thawing it.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:15.009
The journal was just a small diary of research
notes and daily happenings from the researcher.

00:15:15.009 --> 00:15:20.040
It detailed how the Pine Range Research Company
had achieved a huge breakthrough in toilet

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:21.449
paper technology.

00:15:21.449 --> 00:15:26.589
Their new, highly effective toilet paper formula
was somehow infinite - the company had been

00:15:26.589 --> 00:15:28.620
experimenting with anomalies!

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:33.309
They planned to monetize the product and even
expand into other paper products.

00:15:33.309 --> 00:15:37.379
But a few weeks later, the researcher detailed
how the project had hit what he called ‘a

00:15:37.379 --> 00:15:38.660
minor setback’.

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:42.639
They weren’t able to produce cellulose fibers
that make up the paper fast enough to make

00:15:42.639 --> 00:15:44.519
the roll truly infinite.

00:15:44.519 --> 00:15:47.959
It would eventually run out before restocking
itself.

00:15:47.959 --> 00:15:52.769
But he had a solution in mind, though it was
rare and expensive.

00:15:52.769 --> 00:15:57.689
Using a halved isotope of the element hafnium
known as ‘quarternium’, though the Foundation

00:15:57.689 --> 00:16:00.929
hasn’t been able to find any other evidence
of such an element.

00:16:00.929 --> 00:16:05.509
The researcher admitted that he was a botanist,
not a physicist or chemist, but that he was

00:16:05.509 --> 00:16:10.199
perfectly capable of understanding the research
papers written by his colleagues on the matter.

00:16:10.199 --> 00:16:14.809
He claimed that in this day and age, anyone
with a doctorate should be able to understand

00:16:14.809 --> 00:16:17.499
others’ work, if they wrote it clearly enough.

00:16:17.499 --> 00:16:21.019
He was dead-set on figuring out a way to make
his project work.

00:16:21.019 --> 00:16:22.799
And work it did.

00:16:22.799 --> 00:16:27.699
The very next entry detailed how the metallic
housing of the toilet paper was complete.

00:16:27.699 --> 00:16:32.360
The initial roll of toilet paper had been
added in and soon the quarternium alloy would

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:37.899
be complete, for insertion into something
called the ‘hyperbaric containment field’.

00:16:37.899 --> 00:16:42.449
They were still running into issues here and
there, particularly on the matter of the radiation

00:16:42.449 --> 00:16:44.309
that it was constantly emitting.

00:16:44.309 --> 00:16:48.279
But the researcher was confident that they
wouldn’t hamper the rest of the design.

00:16:48.279 --> 00:16:53.549
Unfortunately, from the frost damage, the
remainder of that entry was rendered illegible.

00:16:53.549 --> 00:16:57.480
Another entry the Foundation was able to recover
from the journal was dated to the day before

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.119
they encountered the temperature drop in the
Canadian interior.

00:17:01.119 --> 00:17:05.279
The researcher wrote about how the energy
required for the production of radiation and

00:17:05.279 --> 00:17:10.579
from it, the toilet paper, was higher than
he expected it to be from his calculations.

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:14.860
But he wasn’t worried, and planned to rework
the calculations slightly differently.

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:19.420
He didn’t have time to walk it back for
more extensive testing and modeling, since

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:22.209
the first test of the prototype was tomorrow.

00:17:22.209 --> 00:17:27.829
From what the entry read, he was excited that
the world would never again need toilet paper.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:32.139
The rest of that page was filled with equations,
only some of which the Foundation were able

00:17:32.139 --> 00:17:33.360
to decipher.

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:37.650
But the Foundation mathematicians discovered
what went wrong when they solved the equations

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:43.929
by themselves: the researcher had made a very
simple error early on when calculating e=mc

00:17:43.929 --> 00:17:49.600
squared, which led to his entire work being
thrown off and coming to a conclusion that

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:52.070
the energy output wouldn’t be nearly as
high.

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:56.710
Of course, the researcher was tragically,
and fatally, incorrect.

00:17:56.710 --> 00:18:01.150
His desire to save the world from toilet paper
shortages might have been prescient, given

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:07.340
current events, but his lack of care and patience
led to the deaths of him and all of his coworkers,

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:11.289
and the creation of SCP-2966.

00:18:11.289 --> 00:18:15.370
The Foundation moved in and suppressed the
event with a cover story of a burst liquid

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:20.179
nitrogen tank, and amnestics were disseminated
to any local witnesses.

00:18:20.179 --> 00:18:25.570
Since the Foundation couldn’t safely move
SCP-2966, they chose to convert Pine Range

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:30.620
Research to Outpost 117, and contain it where
it was.

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:34.580
But SCP-2966’s story isn’t quite done
yet.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:39.990
Remember how I mentioned that Foundation personnel
aren’t allowed to try reducing SCP-2966’s

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:41.380
weight since the incident?

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:49.529
Well, a few months after SCP-2966 was contained,
an experiment was performed to see if SCP-2966’s

00:18:49.529 --> 00:18:54.779
mass could be reduced if they pumped it full
of energy while rapidly removing sheets.

00:18:54.779 --> 00:18:59.809
But SCP-2966 immediately went critical, and
exploded, albeit with a much lower yield than

00:18:59.809 --> 00:19:01.830
if it had been fully energized.

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:07.269
Outpost 117 was completely destroyed and all
the staff were killed, though no civilians

00:19:07.269 --> 00:19:08.370
were harmed.

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:14.649
But in the smoking crater, SCP-2966 was recovered
unharmed and significantly lighter.

00:19:14.649 --> 00:19:21.490
Though the Foundation has rebuilt Outpost
117 and recontained SCP-2966, they’ve learned

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:25.710
that simply letting it explode by itself is
not a solution.

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:28.580
This toilet paper just won’t quit.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:35.059
Now go check out “SCP-1984 - Dead Hand”
and “SCP-186 - To End All Wars” for more

00:19:35.059 --> 00:19:36.550
explosive anomalies from SCP Explained!

